@@ -0,0 +1,42 @@
+# Rails and Ruby upgrade notes
+
+## Current release
+
+- Production Ruby: 3.2 (Ruby 3.2.2).
+- Production Rails: 6.1.7.10.
+- The main `Gemfile` remains the Rails 6.1 release manifest.
+- `Gemfile.next` points at Rails 7.0 for dual-boot testing.
+
+## Compatibility decisions
+
+- `config/application.rb` loads Rails 6.1 defaults on Rails 6.1 and Rails 7.0
+  defaults on Rails 7.0.
+- Existing signed cookies and `Rails.application.message_verifier` values are
+  preserved by keeping SHA1 key derivation and signed-cookie digests until the
+  Rails 7 migration can be completed deliberately. Rails 7 uses
+  `config.active_support.key_generator_hash_digest_class = OpenSSL::Digest::SHA1`
+  for this compatibility phase.
+- Cookie serialization uses `:hybrid`, and authenticated cookie/message
+  encryption remains disabled during this staged upgrade. These overrides keep
+  existing browser sessions and the Rails 6.1 rollback format usable on both
+  release branches.
+- Mail links use application message verifiers; the SHA1 compatibility setting
+  lets links generated before the Rails 7 release continue to verify.
+- The Rails 6-only ActionView resolver patch is not loaded under Rails 7, and
+  test date formatting uses `strftime` so fixture data works on both Rails lines.
+
+## Rollback
+
+The Rails 6.1 release is the rollback target for the Ruby 3.2 release. A
+rollback must continue to use the Rails 6.1 Gemfile and `Gemfile.lock`, with
+the compatibility settings recorded above. It must not reload framework
+defaults or change cookie, verifier, or message encryption formats.
+
+## Work for the Rails 7.0 release
+
+- Test production dependencies and plugins with `BUNDLE_GEMFILE=Gemfile.next`.
+- Generate `Gemfile.next.lock` and run the focused mail-link and session tests.
+- Verify Rails 7.0 behavior under Ruby 3.2 before removing compatibility
+  overrides.
+- Plan a one-time cookie/verifier format migration, then remove the SHA1,
+  `:hybrid`, and authenticated-encryption compatibility overrides.
